    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Under the names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jianye</a>, Jiankang, Jiangning and Nanjing, the city was the seat of many regimes from the 3rd century CE, among them Eastern Wu, the Eastern Jin and Southern dynasties, the Southern Tang, the early Ming and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>It was destroyed or plundered several times: by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 589, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> in 976, by the Qing in 1864 after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> capital, and by the Japanese army in 1937, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a> began.</li><li>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> moved the Ming capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1421 Nanjing became a secondary capital, and it grew into a commercial and cultural city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, publishing and examinations.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Boni died during a state visit to the Ming capital in 1408 and was buried in Nanjing, where the Interpreters Institute housed foreign envoys.</p></aside>
    </section>

