    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">229</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> proclaims himself emperor and makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jianye</a> the capital of Eastern Wu</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">317</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin">Sima Rui</a> makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> his capital, and the Eastern Jin dynasty follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">589</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a> orders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> demolished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">937</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bian">Xu Zhigao</a> becomes emperor of the Southern Tang, with Jiangning as capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1368</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> takes the throne as the Hongwu Emperor and names the city Nanjing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1853</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces capture the city and make it their capital, Tianjing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1927</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> National Government declares Nanjing the national capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1937</span><span class="rf-text">Japanese forces take the city on 13 December and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a> begins</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Human habitation in the Nanjing region dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. The type-site of the Beiyinyangying culture, near the modern urban core, contains a cemetery with 225 burials and grave goods such as polished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, jade and agate, which indicate connections with the Lake Tai region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> Hushu culture characterised the Nanjing–Zhenjiang area in the second millennium BCE, and at the Xijie site on the south bank of the Qinhuai River excavations have identified a fortified settlement with concentric ditches and wall foundations from the Shang–Zhou transition. Its relationship to later cities is undetermined.</p><p>Historical records attribute the first named urban foundations in the area to the 5th century BCE: Yuecheng, reportedly built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> of Yue, and Yecheng, attributed to King Fuchai of Wu. Their exact locations and dates remain debated. Under Chu rule the area was known as Jinling, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification it was renamed Moling. The site lies where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> flows past the west and then the north side of the city, and the Qinhuai River runs from south to north through the main urban area to join it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-quan.jpg" alt="Sun Quan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sun_Quan_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yan Liben, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 211 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> moved his headquarters to Moling, and the next year he fortified Shitou Hill to build the Stone City and renamed the region <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jianye</a>. After he proclaimed himself emperor in 229 he established his capital there. Under the Eastern Jin the city was Jiankang, and a rebuilding between 330 and 332 defined its layout for the Southern Dynasties. Canals connected it to regional centres, and commerce centred on four large walled markets plus smaller specialised ones. By the Liang dynasty it housed over 500 monasteries, and in the first half of the 6th century its population likely exceeded 300,000.</p><p>The Ming city had four nested zones: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, the Imperial City, the Capital City and the Outer Enclosure. The Capital City wall exceeded 30 kilometres, and the Outer Enclosure covered approximately 230 square kilometres. By 1391 the permanent population within the Outer Enclosure was estimated at between 900,000 and one million. After the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1421 the registered population fell to between 200,000 and 300,000, but by the mid-Ming period private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>-weaving, fan-making and commercial publishing flourished, and under the Qing the number of private satin looms was estimated to have exceeded 30,000 at its peak.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Nanjing&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-imperial and early imperial eras, Middle Imperial period (589–1368), Late Imperial period (1368–1911), Languages:</p><ul><li>Jun Fang, China's Second Capital – Nanjing Under the Ming, 1368-1644 (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Frederic Jr. Wakeman, The Great Enterprise: The Manchu Reconstruction of Imperial Order in Seventeenth-Century China (University of California Press, 1985)</li><li>John K. Fairbank, The Cambridge History of China: Volume 10, Late Ch'ing 1800–1911, Part 1 (Cambridge University Press, 1978)</li><li>Chuck Wooldridge, City of virtues: Nanjing in an age of utopian visions (Univ. of Washington Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Yuan of Jin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin">Emperor Yuan of Jin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sima_Rui_(Jin_Yuandi).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nanjing was the capital of Eastern Wu (229–280). After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin">Sima Rui</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> his capital in 317, the Eastern Jin and the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Southern Qi, Liang and Chen dynasties ruled southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from it until 589, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a> took the city. For the next three centuries it was a demoted settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bian">Xu Zhigao</a> became emperor of the Southern Tang on 11 November 937, with Jiangning as capital, and the Northern Song took it in 976. Under the Southern Song it was a provisional capital for the fleeing court from May 1129 and kept its prestige as a secondary capital until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took it in 1275.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> captured the city in 1356 and took the throne there in 1368 as the Hongwu Emperor. Zhu Di seized it in 1402, and in 1421 he moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>; from 1441 Nanjing was the secondary capital, with a parallel but mainly symbolic set of ministries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> of Fu was enthroned there in June 1644 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongguang-emperor">Hongguang Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a>, and Dodo's army entered on 6 June 1645. The Qing renamed it Jiangning and made it seat of the Governor-General of Liangjiang. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces held it from 1853 to 1864.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> was sworn in there as provisional president on 1 January 1912, and the government moved to Beijing within weeks. On 1 June 1927 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> government declared it the national capital.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Nanjing&#8221; cites where it covers Six Dynasties period, Middle Imperial period (589–1368), Late Imperial period (1368–1911), Republican era:</p><ul><li>Albert E. Dien, Six dynasties civilization (Yale University Press, 2007)</li><li>Jun Fang, China's Second Capital – Nanjing Under the Ming, 1368-1644 (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Frederic Jr. Wakeman, The Great Enterprise: The Manchu Reconstruction of Imperial Order in Seventeenth-Century China (University of California Press, 1985)</li><li>Albert E. Dien, The Cambridge History of China, Volume 2: The Six Dynasties, 220–589 (Cambridge University Press, 2019)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci.jpg" alt="Matteo Ricci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:利玛窦像，游文辉绘.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chinese brother Emmanuel Pereira (born Yu Wen-hui 游文辉), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> was a major centre of East Asian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and of literary life: the region's vernacular lyrics were codified as the Yuefu corpus "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Wu", and circles such as the "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-friends-of-jingling">Eight Friends of Jingling</a>" met in the late 5th century. Under the Ming the city hosted foreign envoys at the Interpreters Institute, and the Longjiang shipyard built vessels for the Treasure Fleet. In the late 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> settled there; friction with the authorities led to the Nanjing Incident of 1616–1617 and the expulsion of several Jesuits to Macao.</p><p>In 1649 the Qing built a walled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> City in the east, separating the Eight Banner garrison from the Han population's quarter. The Jiangnan examination compound expanded from 7,500 candidates in 1630 to 17,000 by 1850, and Yuan Mei made his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suiyuan">Suiyuan</a> garden a home for women disciples. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a>, residents were segregated by sex into occupational units, and many Buddhist and Taoist temples were destroyed or converted. Missionaries founded Nanking University in 1888 and Drum Tower Hospital in 1892. In the Nanjing Decade the city was home to National Central University, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academia-sinica">Academia Sinica</a> and the Purple Mountain Observatory, while a 1934 survey found that about 45.1 per cent of households lived in shantytowns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-jun">Su Jun</a> Rebellion (327–329) destroyed the imperial palaces. In 548 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> besieged Taicheng for five months, and in 552 loyalist forces defeated him and looted the city. In 589 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> demolished, and its walls, palaces and residences were levelled and the land reclaimed for agriculture. In 976, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> forces besieged the city, Jinling was plundered following Li Yu's surrender, though the Song commander Cao Bin tried to restrain his troops. In March 1853, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces captured the city, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> garrison, its families, Qing officials and local militia suffered heavy casualties through combat, suicide and executions by Taiping forces, who called them "Qing demons". When the city fell in July 1864 after a siege, Qing forces engaged in widespread looting, arson and killing, and destroyed large parts of it, including the Taiping palaces.</p><p>Japanese troops entered Nanjing on 13 December 1937, the day after General Tang Shengzhi ordered a retreat. Over the following weeks they killed civilians and captured soldiers and raped women and girls, and arson destroyed one third of the city. A committee of foreigners led by John Rabe set up the Nanking Safety Zone, credited with saving at least 200,000 lives. The International Military Tribunal for the Far East estimated more than 200,000 dead and the Nanjing War Crimes Tribunal of 1947 more than 300,000; newer estimates run between 100,000 and 200,000. Japanese nationalists and revisionists have denied or minimised the massacre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="Sun Yat-sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">上海波尔照相馆, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> became a frequent subject of classical poetry, and by the late Tang this had developed into a nostalgic tradition called "Meditation on the Past at Jinling". The name Nanjing, "Southern Capital", was formally established in 1368 to distinguish the new capital from the "Northern Capital" at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, and it later served as the counterpart to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. A court language widely believed to be derived from Nanjing speech was used under the Ming and Qing until the mid-nineteenth century, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-chinese">Standard Mandarin</a> was based on Beijing speech, not on that of Nanjing.</p><p>The city is recognised as one of the Four Great Ancient Capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its heritage sites include the Ming city wall, the Ming Xiaoling Mausoleum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> Mausoleum and the Presidential Palace, and its museums include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a> Memorial Hall and the Taiping Kingdom History Museum. The Treaty of Nanking, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, was signed on the British warship HMS Cornwallis at Xiaguan in August 1842.</p><p>After 1949 Nanjing was a province-level municipality, and since 1952 it has been the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>. The first Nanjing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Bridge, designed and built by Chinese engineers, was begun in 1960 and opened in 1968. About 9.4 million people live in the city.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Nanjing, on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, was the capital of Eastern Wu, the Eastern Jin and Southern dynasties, the Southern Tang, the early Ming and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was sacked or demolished several times, and in 1937 Japanese troops carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a>. It is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>.</em></p>

